> I'm going to try some alterations of this with Tourist once I've got 
> Rogue as a sure ascender. (But it's so fun when the beasties get scared 
> and sit there scared while I backstab them to death.  What's up with that?)

What's up with that, indeed?  Look at all those dead Rogues in your
record.  "Sure ascender", yep, I see that now.  

Have you ever considered that your approach may not be as effective as
you think it is?

(Though it does point an odd inconsistency out: why do creatures who are
M1_MINDLESS even get scared in the first place?  Fear is a function of
thought and/or instinct; with no thought, no fear should be possible.)

> Could you consider cutting hunger on skilled and expert casting to 
> unpunish players who waste skill slots on spells?

Why do you consider players who advance spell skills to be "punished"?

When you advance spell skills, you get a reward; the ability to cast
spells in that school with a lower chance of failure (and for some
spells, more powerful effects).

> But this is another case where shield wins over twoweapon.

Melee combat has been redesigned to be more of a three-legged stool:

-- Sword-and-shield allows high to-hit chance, and an extra AC boost.
Downside is low spellcasting due to shield.
-- #twoweapon allows multiple damage types (artifact + silver) and high
damage.  Downside is low to-hit chances.
-- Two-handed weaponry allows extremely high damage and high to-hit chance.
Downside is that if it gets cursed things go completely tits-up.

This is a deliberate change from vanilla, where #twoweapon was the only
worthwhile choice in all circumstances if the character was able to in
the first place.  You appear to be basing your statements on the
assumption that twoweapon will remain "best in all cases" as it used to
be; this is no longer the case in Spork, and never will be again.

In other words, I consider your statement that "this is a case where
shield wins over twoweapon" to be an indicator that my design approach
was successful.

